<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blount Report</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blount Report was the 1893 investigation by James Henderson Blount, sent by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> after the overthrow of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>. Blount concluded that the US minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> and American marines had improperly helped the conspirators. Cleveland withdrew the annexation treaty from the Senate and called for the queen's restoration, but the Provisional Government refused.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blount-report.jpg" alt="Blount Report" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady / Levin Corbin Handy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Henderson_Blount_-_Brady-Handy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In January 1893 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> threatened to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>, forced on the monarchy in 1887, with a constitution restoring power to the throne. American and European resident merchants acting as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a> forced her from power and proclaimed a provisional government, while the American minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> landed marines from the USS Boston in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, ostensibly to protect lives and property. President Benjamin Harrison sent the provisional government's treaty of annexation to the Senate on 15 February 1893; less than three weeks later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a> took office, withdrew it and appointed the former representative James Henderson Blount to investigate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Formally titled Affairs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, the report forms part of the 1893 report of the House of Representatives Foreign Relations Committee on the overthrow. Blount gathered interviews, letters, affidavits and documents such as the Statement of the Hawaiian Patriotic League, though he had no power of subpoena; one historian has noted that he did not interview members of the Committee of Safety and that some questions may have been slanted towards restoring the queen. He delivered the report to Cleveland on 17 July 1893. It concluded that Stevens had acted as a partisan without authority, that the landing of marines under a false or exaggerated pretext had been decisive, and that the revolution ran against the wishes of most of Hawaii's people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On the strength of the report Cleveland dismissed Stevens and worked in secret to restore the queen, on condition of an amnesty for those who had overthrown her. When the new minister, Albert Willis, ordered the provisional government's president, Sanford Dole, to restore her, Dole refused, and Cleveland told Congress that the overthrow had been an act of war committed without the authority of Congress.</p><p>Cleveland then left the matter to the Senate, whose Morgan Report of 1894 contradicted Blount and cleared Stevens and the military; Senator George Gray of Delaware said that witnesses had been examined in a partial and unfair way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a>, who favoured annexation, succeeded Cleveland in 1897, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> was annexed the following year. The Apology Resolution of 1993, which drew on the Blount Report, became a touchstone for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaiian-sovereignty-movement">Hawaiian sovereignty movement</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blount Report” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>President Grover Cleveland withdrew the treaty and, after the <b>Blount Report</b> found American complicity, asked for the queen's restoration.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-hawaii">Provisional Government of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
